course: dictionary
lesson: 10372
title: Words with a history
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell a story about the past. A treadwheel was a huge wheel with steps around its edge. People walked on the steps, and the wheel turned under their feet, but they did not get anywhere. «In many Victorian prisons prisoners did hard labour, such as walking for hours on a treadwheel.» The clues are hard labour and walking for hours, so a treadwheel must be something people walk on to work.
explain: Read the words around the word for more clues. «...a huge turning wheel of steps...» tells you what it looks like. Walking for hours and hard labour tell you it was a punishment, not a game. Test your meaning in the sentence: "Prisoners walked for hours on a huge turning wheel of steps" still makes sense, so your meaning is right.
explain: Words can also be used in a picture sense. The sentence tells you that «treadmill» comes from treadwheel. Today a treadmill is a running machine in a gym, and people also say «a treadmill» for boring work that never seems to end. So treadwheel can mean a wheel, a punishment, or dull endless work, and the sentence around it shows which one.
fact: A treadwheel is a large wheel turned by people or animals walking on steps inside or around it.
fact: Treadwheels were used in the past to grind corn or pump water.
fact: «Treadwheel» was also a punishment in Victorian prisons, where prisoners walked on one for hours.
fact: In the figurative sense, «a treadmill» means boring, tiring work that never seems to end.
fact: In The Wind in the Willows, Toad fears hard punishment in gaol.
exercise: «In many Victorian prisons prisoners did hard labour, such as walking for hours on a treadwheel.» What is a treadwheel? | A cup of hot tea | A tall prison wall with a gate | A horse-drawn carriage used by prisoners | *A huge turning wheel of steps that people walk on
exercise: Dad says his homework is a treadmill. What does he mean? | He is running on a machine at the gym | He is building a wooden wheel | *Boring, tiring work that never seems to end | He is walking up a steep hill
exercise: In the past, what was a treadwheel used for? | To mend broken roads | To light the streets at night | To carry letters to far-off towns | *To grind corn or pump water
test: Why was the treadwheel a punishment in Victorian prisons? | *Prisoners walked on it for hours, often doing no useful work | Prisoners were given sweets for walking on it | Prisoners used it to grow vegetables | Prisoners rode it home at the end of the day
test: Ruby says, «Every day is a treadmill of chores.» What does treadmill mean here? | A large wooden wheel for grinding corn | A running machine in a gym | *Boring work that never seems to end | A kind of small boat
exam: Which is the best way to work out a new word like treadwheel? | *Read the words around it and try your meaning in the sentence | Skip the sentence and guess from the first letter | Ask someone to say the word aloud and copy it | Choose the longest answer on the page
